  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@5.13.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet"/>
  <style>
    @font-face {
      font-family: "MyBrand";
      src: url("/fonts/MyBrand-Regular.woff2") format("woff2");
      font-weight: 400;
      font-style: normal;
    }
    @font-face {
      font-family: "MyBrand";
      src: url("/fonts/MyBrand-Bold.woff2") format("woff2");
      font-weight: 700;
      font-style: normal;
    }
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script type="module">
  import maplibregl from 'https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@5.13.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js';

Below is a **step‑by‑step recipe** for adding a **hillshade layer** that is driven by a **raster‑DEM** source to a MapLibre GL JS map.  
Everything is written for the current stable version (≥ 5.11.0) but works with older releases as well – just drop the optional `terrain`‑related bits if you’re on a pre‑5.x build.

---

## 1. Overview of the pieces you need

| Piece | What it does | Required? |
|------|---------------|-----------|
| **Raster‑DEM source** (`type: "raster-dem"`) | Supplies elevation data (in meters) as a raster image. | ✅ |
| **Hillshade layer** (`type: "hillshade"`) | Renders a shaded relief image from the DEM. | ✅ |
| **Optional terrain source** (`type: "terrain"`) | Lets you “extrude” the terrain in the 3‑D view (pitch/rotate). Not needed for the 2‑D hillshade itself, but often paired with it. | ❌ (optional) |
| **Map style** | Holds the source and layer definitions. | ✅ |
| **CORS / hosting** | The DEM tiles must be reachable from the browser (CORS headers or same‑origin). | ✅ |

---

## 2. Prepare (or pick) a DEM tile set

MapLibre can consume any raster tile service that returns **single‑band 16‑bit elevation** (or 8‑bit if you’re okay with lower precision). Common sources:

| Provider | URL template example | Notes |
|----------|----------------------|-------|
| **MapTiler DEM** | `https://api.maptiler.com/tiles/terrain-tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.png?key=YOUR_KEY` | 16‑bit PNG, free tier up to 100 k tiles. |
| **OpenTopoMap DEM** | `https://{s}.tile.opentopomap.org/DEM/{z}/{x}/{y}.png` | 8‑bit, public, but limited coverage. |
| **Self‑hosted MBTiles** | `https://myserver.com/tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.png` | Must serve `Content-Type: image/png` and `Access-Control-Allow-Origin:*`. |
| **Mapbox Terrain‑RGB** (if you have a token) | `https://api.mapbox.com/v4/mapbox.terrain-rgb/{z}/{x}/{y}@2x.pngraw?access_token=YOUR_TOKEN` | 24‑bit RGB encoding; you’ll need to set `encoding: "terrarium"` (see below). |

> **Tip:** If you already have a vector‑tiles style (e.g. from OpenMapTiles) you can reuse its `tiles` URL and just change the `type` to `"raster-dem"`.

---

## 3. Add the DEM source to the style

```js
// 1️⃣ Create the map (basic example)
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',               // HTML element id
  style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json', // any base style
  center: [0, 0],
  zoom: 2
});

// 2️⃣ When the style is loaded, add the DEM source
map.on('style.load', () => {
  map.addSource('my-dem', {
    type: 'raster-dem',
    // The URL template for your DEM tiles
    tiles: [
      'https://api.maptiler.com/tiles/terrain-tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.png?key=YOUR_KEY'
    ],
    // Optional: tell MapLibre how the elevation is encoded.
    //   * "mapbox"   – Mapbox Terrain‑RGB (24‑bit)
    //   * "terrarium" – Terrarium (16‑bit, 0‑255 per channel)
    //   * "mapbox" is the default; omit if you use plain 16‑bit PNG.
    // encoding: 'mapbox',   // only needed for Terrain‑RGB
    tileSize: 256,          // most DEM services use 256 px tiles
    // If your DEM tiles are hosted on a different origin,
    // make sure they send CORS headers (or use a proxy).
    // Example: `maxzoom: 14, minzoom: 0` – adjust to your source.
    maxzoom: 14,
    minzoom: 0
  });
});
```

### Important source options

| Option | Meaning |
|--------|---------|
| `tiles` | Array of URL templates (you can list several subdomains). |
| `tileSize` | Usually `256`. If you serve 512 px tiles, set `512`. |
| `encoding` | Only for Mapbox Terrain‑RGB (`"mapbox"`). For plain 16‑bit PNG you can omit it. |
| `bounds` | Optional – helps the map know the DEM’s geographic extent. |
| `maxzoom` / `minzoom` | Must match the source’s capabilities; otherwise you’ll see “tile outside zoom range” warnings. |

---

## 4. Add the hillshade layer

```js
// 3️⃣ Add the hillshade layer (still inside the style.load handler)
map.addLayer({
  id: 'hillshade',
  type: 'hillshade',
  source: 'my-dem',
  // The hillshade layer does **not** need a `source-layer` because raster sources have no layers.
  paint: {
    // These three paint properties control the visual appearance.
    // Feel free to tweak them to match your design.
    'hillshade-illumination-direction': 315, // Sun direction (degrees clockwise from north)
    'hillshade-illumination-anchor': 'map', // Keep sun direction fixed to the map (instead of viewport)
    'hillshade-exaggeration': 0.5,          // 0 = flat, 1 = default, >1 = more contrast
    // Optional colour tweaks:
    // 'hillshade-shadow-color': '#000000',
    // 'hillshade-highlight-color': '#ffffff',
    // 'hillshade-accent-color': '#bbbbbb'
  }
});
```

### Paint properties explained

| Property | Default | Typical range | What it does |
|----------|---------|---------------|--------------|
| `hillshade-illumination-direction` | `315` | `0‑360` (degrees) | Sun azimuth. 315° = NW (classic “hillshade” look). |
| `hillshade-illumination-anchor` | `"viewport"` | `"map"` or `"viewport"` | `"map"` keeps the sun fixed to geographic north; `"viewport"` rotates with the map. |
| `hillshade-exaggeration` | `0.5` | `0‑2` (or higher) | Controls contrast; higher values make shadows deeper. |
| `hillshade-shadow-color` | `#000000` | any CSS colour | Colour of the shadows. |
| `hillshade-highlight-color` | `#ffffff` | any CSS colour | Colour of the illuminated slopes. |
| `hillshade-accent-color` | `#bbbbbb` | any CSS colour | Mid‑tone “ambient” shading. |

---

## 5. (Optional) Add 3‑D terrain – makes the hillshade look even richer

If you want the map to **tilt** and **pitch** and show true 3‑D terrain (the DEM is used for vertex displacement), add a **terrain source** that points at the same DEM:

```js
// 4️⃣ Add terrain (optional)
map.setTerrain({
  source: 'my-dem',   // reuse the raster-dem source
  exaggeration: 1.5   // make mountains look taller (optional)
});

// Enable 3‑D interaction
map.addControl(new maplibregl.NavigationControl({ visualizePitch: true }));
```

> **Caution:** The hillshade layer will still render *on top* of the terrain surface. If you want the hillshade to be *under* the terrain (i.e., the terrain geometry already contains the shading), you can **omit the hillshade layer** and rely on the built‑in terrain shading that MapLibre adds automatically. The explicit hillshade layer is useful when you need a custom look or want the shading even when the map is flat (pitch = 0).

---

## 6. Full working example (HTML)

```html
<!doctype html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre GL JS – Hillshade from raster‑DEM</title>
  <meta name="viewport" content="initial-scale=1,maximum-scale=1,user-scalable=no" />
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@5.11.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body { margin:0; padding:0; }
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@5.11.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
<script>
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {
        // a simple background source so the map has something to draw under the hillshade
        "osm": {
          type: "raster",
          tiles: ["https://a.tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png"],
          tileSize: 256,
          attribution: "© OpenStreetMap contributors"
        }
      },
      layers: [
        { id: "osm", type: "raster", source: "osm" }
      ]
    },
    center: [13.4, 52.5],
    zoom: 6,
    pitch: 45,
    bearing: -17
  });

  map.on('style.load', () => {
    // 1️⃣ DEM source
    map.addSource('my-dem', {
      type: 'raster-dem',
      tiles: [
        // Replace with your own DEM endpoint
        'https://api.maptiler.com/tiles/terrain-tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.png?key=YOUR_KEY'
      ],
      tileSize: 256,
      maxzoom: 14,
      minzoom: 0
    });

    // 2️⃣ Hillshade layer
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'hillshade',
      type: 'hillshade',
      source: 'my-dem',
      paint: {
        'hillshade-illumination-direction': 315,
        'hillshade-illumination-anchor': 'map',
        'hillshade-exaggeration': 0.7
      }
    });

    // 3️⃣ (Optional) 3‑D terrain
    map.setTerrain({ source: 'my-dem', exaggeration: 1.2 });
    map.addControl(new maplibregl.NavigationControl({ visualizePitch: true }));
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

*Replace `YOUR_KEY` with a valid MapTiler key (or point the `tiles` array at your own server).*

---

## 7. Common pitfalls & how to fix them

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|---------|--------------|-----|
| **No hillshade appears (just the base raster)** | DEM source not added **before** the hillshade layer, or `source` name typo. | Verify `map.addSource('my-dem', …)` runs before `map.addLayer({ source: 'my-dem' })`. |
| **Console shows `Failed to load tile …`** | CORS missing, wrong URL, or tile out of range. | Ensure the DEM server sends `Access-Control-Allow-Origin:*` (or same‑origin). Check `maxzoom/minzoom`. |
| **Hillshade looks flat / no contrast** | `hillshade-exaggeration` is too low (default 0.5). | Increase to `1` or higher. |
| **CJK characters look wrong** | Not a hillshade issue – but if you also have label layers, remember that CJK is rendered locally by default (`localIdeographFontFamily`). | Set `localIdeographFontFamily` to a nicer on‑device CJK font, or serve CJK glyph PBFs and set `localIdeographFontFamily: false`. |
| **Terrain appears but hillshade disappears** | The hillshade layer is being **occluded** by the terrain surface (the terrain draws *after* raster layers). | Either (a) move the hillshade **below** the terrain by adding it **before** `setTerrain`, or (b) drop the hillshade layer and rely on the built‑in terrain shading. |
| **Shadows point the wrong way** | `hillshade-illumination-direction` is set incorrectly or you’re using `"viewport"` anchor while rotating the map. | Use `"map"` anchor for a fixed sun direction, or adjust the direction value. |

---

## 8. Performance considerations

| Factor | Impact | Recommendation |
|--------|--------|----------------|
| **Tile size** (`tileSize`) | Larger tiles (512 px) reduce request count but increase per‑tile payload. | Stick with 256 px unless your server is optimized for larger tiles. |
| **Zoom range** (`maxzoom`) | Higher maxzoom → many more tiles at close zoom → more network traffic. | Limit `maxzoom` to the highest resolution you
